expl('EXP064','INC120','Conflation of the Echo events (16 Mar), the documented Belt sighting (24 Mar) and routine multi-site restart nights; hangar rumor ("UFO") spreading before any official word; memory of dates and missile counts drifting over 37 years; Echo experience of a spring-1967 shutdown transposed to Oscar','Salas, Jamison and Klotz disagree on date and number (Klotz asks for documents); Evans recalls nothing for Oscar on 16 Mar; the wing history documents one shutdown; Jamison saw no UFO and was not asked to sign anything','Belt (24 Mar) is documented in Blue Book and the Tribune with Malmstrom helicopter/ground search next morning, consistent with Jamison hearing about it; several witnesses (Koertner, Jamison, Meiwald) recall an Oscar event','OPEN',10560)
expl('EXP065','INC119','Echo-2 sighting after the 0845 shutdown (camper team incident in the afternoon or evening) is not a cause of the shutdown; ring of lights around a perimeter light in falling snow (halo or lens effect) and later rumor attached to the shutdown','Wing history gives the shutdown at 0845 and power events at ~1450; Barlow places the camper incident after his team was recalled and says he was told later at Echo-1','Barlow reports confiscated film and SAC brass at Echo-1','OPEN',10452)
ev('EVD129',"Cross-check of the Barlow, Mr. X and Jamison accounts against the wing history (SRC002, EVD085) and the Belt record. (a) Timing: SRC002 puts the Echo shutdown at 0845 on 16 Mar; Barlow says Job Control called his team back 'on the day we were supposed to return' and the camper incident was 'earlier that afternoon', so by his own sequence it followed the shutdown rather than caused it; Mr. X (E-7, dusk restart) is also after the event. (b) VRSA: SRC002 records VRSA channels 9 and 12 No-Go at every LF; Barlow says nothing showed on VRSA at the sites (CLM245 contradicted at LCC level; at the LFs he may mean the LF status unit) and Mr. X says Channel 11 DC Coupler, which matches neither 9 nor 12. (c) Echo E-7 was in fact sampled for channel 50 data (SRC002), consistent with a team working there that day. (d) Oscar: Jamison, Koertner and Meiwald each recall an Oscar event; the wing history records none; Salas's argument that the Air Force suppressed Oscar is untestable and Evans recalls nothing for 16 Mar. (e) Belt: the 24 Mar 1967 Belt report (trucker Ken Williams, Great Falls Tribune 25 and 29 Mar, Blue Book; Malmstrom planned a helicopter/ground search the next morning, per search results) corroborates Jamison's overheard radio talk about a UFO in a canyon on or about 24 Mar but not the missile outage. (f) Jamison saw no UFO and signed no NDA, and said teams got a special UFO briefing for two weeks; no document for any of this was found.",'ANALYST_COMPARISON','SRC002; INC022; search results','INC119;INC120;INC121;CLM245;CLM247','10415','HIGH','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD130',"Telstar check (CLM242): Telstar 1 and 2 were small (about 34-inch) experimental communications satellites in elliptical orbits; they would have been faint, steady-moving points, not a bright light with right-angle turns, so Hastings's rejection of the 'Telstar' explanation for Hughes's observations is reasonable as a description of what satellites do; the observation itself is unverified and unsourced beyond one 2003 email (general knowledge, not rechecked this session).",'ANALYST_ASSESSMENT','general knowledge','CLM242;INC116','9724','LOW','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
